WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:10.020
&gt;&gt;David&nbsp;Prior: Well, welcome everybody to
the public board meeting of the NHS. I have

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:17.440
apologies for absence from Julian&nbsp;Kelly and
Matt&nbsp;Style is stepping in for him. Does anyone

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:25.259
have any declarations of interest that they
would like to mention? No?

00:00:25.259 --> 00:00:29.390
Is everyone happy with the minutes of the
last board meeting, which we held on 25th&nbsp;June?

00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:36.910
Okay, thank you very much.
So, Simon, I am handing over to you for your

00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:41.160
Chief&nbsp;Executive's report.
&gt;&gt;Simon&nbsp;Stevens: Thank you very much David.

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:48.060
The board has put in this extra meeting because
we knew that at this time we would be pivoting

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:54.950
between dealing with the ongoing coronavirus
response and the restart of other services

00:00:54.950 --> 00:01:01.190
across the NHS, as well as ensuring that work
was underway to prepare for extra demands

00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:05.750
that might be placed on the NHS in the event
of a second spike and going into the winter

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:12.370
period. So the main business before us this
morning is to take track of the work that

00:01:12.370 --> 00:01:18.040
has been done on those fronts. We are coming
down, obviously continuing to come down, from

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:25.780
the peak of inpatient emergency coronavirus
pressures, 933&nbsp;COVID‑positive inpatients

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:32.600
across the English NHS hospitals as we speak
and so, as we discussed at our last public

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:38.560
board meeting a month ago, by the end of this
week, 31st&nbsp;July, we will make a determination

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:45.229
as to whether or not it's appropriate to step
down from the level&nbsp;four EPRR national incident,

00:01:45.229 --> 00:01:51.979
while nevertheless standing ready to re‑escalate
should circumstances require. And we will

00:01:51.979 --> 00:01:59.740
capture that in the so‑called phase‑three
letter that we will be sending to the NHS

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:04.140
following the discussion that we have at the
board this afternoon to finalise what that

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:12.330
should look like and as part of that, Amanda
and I and colleagues have been meeting virtually

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:20.099
with our colleagues across seven&nbsp;regions
in the NHS, from trusts and from CCGs, integrated

00:02:20.099 --> 00:02:25.719
care systems and also with a number of the
national patients groups representing the

00:02:25.719 --> 00:02:31.150
public and patients in areas such as mental
health, cancer, older people's services, to

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:35.489
make sure that their perspectives are built
into what we're doing.

00:02:35.489 --> 00:02:41.709
We also expect that within the next several
days we will be publishing the long‑heralded

00:02:41.709 --> 00:02:50.480
2020–2021 version of the NHS&nbsp;People&nbsp;Plan.
I particularly want to thank Prerana and colleagues

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:58.599
for their work on that and looking ahead to
our public meetings in October, 1st&nbsp;October,

00:02:58.599 --> 00:03:04.530
that's when we will also be bringing forward
an NHS&nbsp;England and improvement response to

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:13.109
the very important report that Baroness&nbsp;Cumberlege
published a few weeks ago and which the Department&nbsp;of&nbsp;Health&nbsp;and&nbsp;Social&nbsp;Care

00:03:13.109 --> 00:03:17.719
are coordinating the cross‑sectoral response
to in the meantime.

00:03:17.719 --> 00:03:24.409
I do also just want to say that, as well as
dealing with those immediate here‑and‑now

00:03:24.409 --> 00:03:29.939
questions for the balance of the year, the
debate on coronavirus and what needs to be

00:03:29.939 --> 00:03:37.069
learned has also served as a focusing moment
for some of the bigger discussions that have

00:03:37.069 --> 00:03:44.299
got to be got right. So, on social care, we
are, I think, beginning to see increasing

00:03:44.299 --> 00:03:48.829
urgency around the need to provide proper
support for the social care sector, which

00:03:48.829 --> 00:03:54.909
is obviously something the NHS supports in
its own right and it strongly relies on getting

00:03:54.909 --> 00:04:04.400
right, looking out over the autumn and winter.
On obesity, this past weekend and into early

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:08.989
this week, good progress being made on some
of the wider measures that have been in debate

00:04:08.989 --> 00:04:15.430
for at least five&nbsp;years and as part of that,
an enhanced NHS response, including widening

00:04:15.430 --> 00:04:21.280
the diabetes prevention programme and other
ways in which the NHS will play our role.

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:28.590
And finally, on inequalities and the way in
which coronavirus has multiplied and exploited

00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:35.000
those and their impact on health, considering
particularly around race and health, I want

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:40.740
to use this opportunity this afternoon just
to announce that, as discussed, we have made

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:44.919
an appointment now to chair – the inaugural
chair of the Race and Health Observatory that

00:04:44.919 --> 00:04:51.389
is being hosted by the NHS&nbsp;Confederation.
Following open advert, that post will now

00:04:51.389 --> 00:04:58.590
be held by Marie&nbsp;Gabriel, a very experienced
NHS and public service leader. Similarly,

00:04:58.590 --> 00:05:09.020
Prerana&nbsp;Issar has appointed, to our own Equality
and Diversity and Inclusion team Jenny&nbsp;Douglas‑Todd

00:05:09.020 --> 00:05:15.740
and Mike&nbsp;Franklin, also following public
advertisement, experienced leaders from outside

00:05:15.740 --> 00:05:21.139
of the NHS per se and they're complementing
the work that Owen&nbsp;Williams, the Chief&nbsp;Executive

00:05:21.139 --> 00:05:26.340
of Calderdale&nbsp;and&nbsp;Huddersfield NHS&nbsp;Trust
is leading with Ian&nbsp;Dodge and colleagues

00:05:26.340 --> 00:05:30.520
on building in the race lens into the health
and equalities work that we are committed

00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:36.129
to advancing this year and beyond.
And finally, we are this afternoon publishing

00:05:36.129 --> 00:05:42.960
the 2019 version of the arm's length body
Workforce&nbsp;Race&nbsp;Equality&nbsp;Standard measures,

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:49.039
as well as a new set of proposals for measuring
race inequality in the medical profession,

00:05:49.039 --> 00:05:52.229
the Medical&nbsp;RES.
So, I think, everything else, David, will

00:05:52.229 --> 00:05:56.199
be covered later in our agenda but that perhaps
sets up the discussion for this afternoon.

00:05:56.199 --> 00:05:59.830
&gt;&gt;David&nbsp;Prior: Good, thank you very much,
Simon. Did anyone on the board want to have

00:05:59.830 --> 00:06:04.990
any question to Simon before we move on to
the sort of operating performance of the NHS?

00:06:04.990 --> 00:06:12.270
Okay, let's move straight on, then, to Amanda
and Matt&nbsp;Style, who are going to present

00:06:12.270 --> 00:06:17.240
the operational performance report. Amanda?
&gt;&gt;Amanda&nbsp;Pritchard: Thank you, David. So,

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:22.571
if I just give the headlines, hand over to
Matt to talk about the financial performance

00:06:22.571 --> 00:06:27.039
and then, of course, welcome any questions
and indeed I know that Pauline&nbsp;Philip is

00:06:27.039 --> 00:06:31.639
with us and more than happy to talk about
some of the detail, if that's helpful.

00:06:31.639 --> 00:06:38.379
So, just to say clearly we're aware that we've
had a completely kind of extraordinary experience

00:06:38.379 --> 00:06:44.080
over the last few months and I think, you
know, again, just going out and about over

00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:49.780
the last few weeks, beginning to reconnect
with people on the frontline, just struck

00:06:49.780 --> 00:06:55.250
again by what an extraordinary response it
really has been across the whole of the NHS.

00:06:55.250 --> 00:07:00.419
That's true – it's true of primary care
as it is of mental health, specialised services

00:07:00.419 --> 00:07:04.360
across the board.
But what we did see – and I know we've talked

00:07:04.360 --> 00:07:10.139
about this before, of course – was a significant
drop‑off in the sort of usual demand for

00:07:10.139 --> 00:07:19.460
services in that early period of the COVID
peak but we're now beginning to see services

00:07:19.460 --> 00:07:25.610
absolutely start to move back to, or towards,
pre‑COVID levels of activity and indeed

00:07:25.610 --> 00:07:28.569
demand beginning to move in that direction
as well.

00:07:28.569 --> 00:07:34.909
So, just to be specific for a moment, as you
know we saw a drop‑off in A&amp;E attendances

00:07:34.909 --> 00:07:40.940
and emergency admissions at the beginning
of the COVID surge. Predominantly, those were

00:07:40.940 --> 00:07:46.650
patients with minor conditions but we are
now seeing an upturn, an increase in demand,

00:07:46.650 --> 00:07:51.120
at the front door for urgent emergency care.
Performance against a four‑hour standard

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:57.629
continues to be very strong, linked, of course,
to a much reduced level of demand.

00:07:57.629 --> 00:08:04.159
111 – and we'll talk about that later – continues
to be very busy and has been a critical part

00:08:04.159 --> 00:08:09.789
of our ability to support patients through
the COVID response and I think really, again,

00:08:09.789 --> 00:08:17.639
one of the many areas where actually we've
been able to innovate to respond at pace and

00:08:17.639 --> 00:08:24.439
in a way that has been very complementary
to the rest of the service offer over this

00:08:24.439 --> 00:08:29.330
period.
Moving on to referral to treatment, elective

00:08:29.330 --> 00:08:35.010
care. So, what we've got at the moment is,
of course, a declining number of people actually

00:08:35.010 --> 00:08:41.890
on the waiting list but we are seeing an increase
in the time that patients are waiting and

00:08:41.890 --> 00:08:48.060
that, of course, is connected with the reduction
in routine activity that was performed over

00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:55.700
the last period. So we are now at a much higher
number of patients waiting more than 18&nbsp;weeks,

00:08:55.700 --> 00:09:00.690
waiting indeed more than 52&nbsp;weeks, than I
think anybody would be comfortable with. So

00:09:00.690 --> 00:09:04.490
the big focus, of course, for the NHS – and
we'll talk about that more in this meeting

00:09:04.490 --> 00:09:11.690
– as we really lean into the recovery, not
just of our urgent services but actually increasingly

00:09:11.690 --> 00:09:18.700
now of our routine services – is to make
sure that we have achieved that kind of restoration

00:09:18.700 --> 00:09:24.680
of elective care to deal with both the urgent
and the longer‑waiting patients.

00:09:24.680 --> 00:09:28.980
We've been really encouraged by the fact that
more people are now coming forward for cancer

00:09:28.980 --> 00:09:35.880
care, so that help us to help you campaign
– I just really want to encourage anyone

00:09:35.880 --> 00:09:41.130
– continue to encourage anyone who has concerns
that they might have symptoms that could be

00:09:41.130 --> 00:09:47.460
indicative of cancer to seek help, because
the NHS remains completely open to business,

00:09:47.460 --> 00:09:53.120
where we need to be and particularly for patients
on cancer pathways.

00:09:53.120 --> 00:09:57.900
I think we've talked about it before but we
have developed hubs for cancer surgery. Now

00:09:57.900 --> 00:10:04.040
all 21 cancer alliances have them up and running
and they have enabled us to maintain a high

00:10:04.040 --> 00:10:09.180
level of cancer treatment through the whole
of the COVID period.

00:10:09.180 --> 00:10:13.220
I won't say anymore specifically about elective
care, although obviously happy to talk more

00:10:13.220 --> 00:10:19.280
about it if that's helpful. We are seeing,
I think – worth saying – on primary care

00:10:19.280 --> 00:10:26.220
– the data is slightly less straightforward
to interpret on primary care and we know it

00:10:26.220 --> 00:10:31.370
doesn't give us quite as accurate a picture
but again, what we are seeing is an increase

00:10:31.370 --> 00:10:40.300
in demand for GP appointments, both face to
face and online or by telephone and again,

00:10:40.300 --> 00:10:47.540
it's been an enormous shift away from face‑to‑face
to digital ways of providing care that we've

00:10:47.540 --> 00:10:51.600
seen in primary care, has been echoed in mental
health and community and in secondary care,

00:10:51.600 --> 00:10:57.630
which has allowed us to continue to operate
at a high level of activity despite some of

00:10:57.630 --> 00:11:03.420
the COVID restrictions and really keen to
continue to encourage the use of those digital

00:11:03.420 --> 00:11:06.580
routes, although, of course, as Simon said
a moment ago, one of the things we are very

00:11:06.580 --> 00:11:11.630
keen to do is make sure that we're really
looking at that through an equalities lens,

00:11:11.630 --> 00:11:16.620
to make sure that it's not excluding anybody
from accessing services when they need it

00:11:16.620 --> 00:11:24.490
but actually is a genuinely additional service,
for those who – for whom it is a good option.

00:11:24.490 --> 00:11:31.820
A final couple of things to say: mental health.
We have maintained the commitment to both

00:11:31.820 --> 00:11:37.580
the investment in mental health and continued
the absolute commitment to achieving the same

00:11:37.580 --> 00:11:42.910
outcomes in mental health services throughout
the COVID period and what we've seen is a

00:11:42.910 --> 00:11:49.370
huge amount of adaptation in service provision,
again, an enormous innovation across mental

00:11:49.370 --> 00:11:55.010
health. But we are aware, I think and have
been throughout that there was likely to be

00:11:55.010 --> 00:12:01.120
an increase in demand, both from the population
and indeed from support for our own staff

00:12:01.120 --> 00:12:05.580
for mental health support on the back of what
they've experienced these last few months

00:12:05.580 --> 00:12:11.270
and we are beginning to see a definite increase
in demand for mental health services.

00:12:11.270 --> 00:12:19.250
So, a huge amount done to maintain services
but definitely beginning to see demand coming

00:12:19.250 --> 00:12:23.140
through.
Final thing I just wanted to say was really

00:12:23.140 --> 00:12:28.310
just to draw the board's attention to the
fourth annual leader report, which was published

00:12:28.310 --> 00:12:35.710
by the University&nbsp;of&nbsp;Bristol on 16th&nbsp;July.
That is – it's – we published our leader

00:12:35.710 --> 00:12:40.440
learning from action report at the same time
and that's a really important piece of work

00:12:40.440 --> 00:12:46.220
because it brings together a lot of the learning
around people with a learning disability and/or

00:12:46.220 --> 00:12:52.390
autism.
There's some quite challenging findings and

00:12:52.390 --> 00:13:01.800
we, I think, feel absolutely a sense of continued
commitment and the importance of not losing

00:13:01.800 --> 00:13:08.800
sight of what is a very ambitious work plan
around this area, so absolutely maintaining

00:13:08.800 --> 00:13:15.630
that focus on the care, education and treatment
reviews, absolutely maintaining a focus on

00:13:15.630 --> 00:13:21.220
the leader reviews and continuing, of course,
to support people to move away from inpatient

00:13:21.220 --> 00:13:26.550
settings, where that's appropriate. So those
– where that's possible and appropriate

00:13:26.550 --> 00:13:29.900
and indeed, of course, the challenge to us
is to make sure it can be.

00:13:29.900 --> 00:13:35.630
But just that – well, in fact, you know,
that's probably enough, isn't it? Let me hand

00:13:35.630 --> 00:13:38.670
over to Matt and then we can take questions
if helpful.

00:13:38.670 --> 00:13:46.090
&gt;&gt;David&nbsp;Prior: Thank you Amanda. Matt?
&gt;&gt;Matt&nbsp;Style: Thank you, Chair. So, as colleagues

00:13:46.090 --> 00:13:51.740
are aware, we have, since April, been operating
a simplified financial framework across the

00:13:51.740 --> 00:13:57.890
NHS, effectively comprising block contract
values to cover the sort of pre‑COVID cost

00:13:57.890 --> 00:14:04.080
base plus retrospective top‑ups to ensure
that the resources were available in a timely

00:14:04.080 --> 00:14:09.980
fashion to allow the NHS to respond to the
pandemic.

00:14:09.980 --> 00:14:16.210
The paper presents the financial position
at month two in the context of that revised

00:14:16.210 --> 00:14:23.740
financial framework. On a year‑to‑date
basis at month&nbsp;two, we have incurred additional

00:14:23.740 --> 00:14:31.160
COVID‑related costs of around £3&nbsp;billion,
broadly speaking, split 50‑50 between providers

00:14:31.160 --> 00:14:38.390
and commissioners.
Those – that includes, in the provider sector,

00:14:38.390 --> 00:14:45.170
lost income, so principally lost income from
private patients, commercial income, local

00:14:45.170 --> 00:14:51.750
authority income and research income. It includes
additional pay costs and additional non‑pay

00:14:51.750 --> 00:14:58.160
costs, drugs and clinical supplies being probably
the largest elements within that.

00:14:58.160 --> 00:15:06.650
After offsetting savings, the overall year‑to‑date
overspend has been £2.6&nbsp;billion. These figures

00:15:06.650 --> 00:15:11.580
do not take account of some costs which are
being borne separately and nationally by the

00:15:11.580 --> 00:15:16.620
Department&nbsp;of&nbsp;Health&nbsp;and&nbsp;Social&nbsp;Care.
As its presented – as I've just presented

00:15:16.620 --> 00:15:21.310
it and as it's set out in the paper, as I
say, we're showing an overspend but just to

00:15:21.310 --> 00:15:28.690
be clear with the board, arrangements are
in place to ensure that those costs are met

00:15:28.690 --> 00:15:37.950
and funded by the exchequer and the expenditure
is in line with the arrangements we've made

00:15:37.950 --> 00:15:43.320
with the government to cover this – the
cost of COVID over the course of this year.

00:15:43.320 --> 00:15:49.181
And I think I'll leave it there.
&gt;&gt;David&nbsp;Prior: Thanks very much, Matt. Any

00:15:49.181 --> 00:15:53.861
questions for Amanda and Matt?
&gt;&gt;Wol&nbsp;Kolade: David, can I come in, it's

00:15:53.861 --> 00:15:57.690
Wol?
&gt;&gt;David&nbsp;Prior: Yeah, do, Wol, yeah.

00:15:57.690 --> 00:16:04.990
&gt;&gt;Wol&nbsp;Kolade: So, Amanda, thank you very
much for your update on how the services are

00:16:04.990 --> 00:16:09.870
responding and recalibrating. Is there more
we can do, though, to get the demand side

00:16:09.870 --> 00:16:15.530
really moving on so that, actually, the underlying
waiting list starts to reflect – be a true

00:16:15.530 --> 00:16:18.790
reflection of what's going on?
I mean there's mention of the fact that the

00:16:18.790 --> 00:16:26.310
clinical infection rate has gone crashing
down and I just don't think this is in the

00:16:26.310 --> 00:16:30.160
public mindset; is there more we can do there?
&gt;&gt;Amanda&nbsp;Pritchard: Thank you, Wol. I think

00:16:30.160 --> 00:16:34.240
that's a – do you know, I think this is
an incredibly important point because one

00:16:34.240 --> 00:16:39.420
of the things that we're really conscious
of is that referral rates have not bounced

00:16:39.420 --> 00:16:45.150
back to anything like pre‑COVID levels,
so there is, I think, a genuine risk that

00:16:45.150 --> 00:16:49.520
what we – while we've got a line of sight
to those patients who are in the system, we

00:16:49.520 --> 00:16:52.550
don't have a line of sight to those people
who've not yet presented.

00:16:52.550 --> 00:16:57.490
So clearly we do have campaigns like the help
us to help you campaign, we're working closely

00:16:57.490 --> 00:17:02.950
with colleagues in PHE, DHSC and others around
the kind of public information but I think

00:17:02.950 --> 00:17:08.800
one of the big learning points, certainly
from the – for example the outbreak in Leicester,

00:17:08.800 --> 00:17:15.540
or the increasing prevalence of COVID in Leicester
is actually how important getting those messages

00:17:15.540 --> 00:17:19.400
out to local communities from trusted local
leaders is.

00:17:19.400 --> 00:17:23.800
And so it's not to say that there isn't an
important role to play for national campaigns,

00:17:23.800 --> 00:17:28.240
I think there is and I think we need to keep
doing that but I think we need to really complement

00:17:28.240 --> 00:17:36.110
that with CCG‑led, local authority‑led,
local NHS‑led messaging, including direct

00:17:36.110 --> 00:17:40.700
contact, of course, between clinicians and
their patients, that encourages people who

00:17:40.700 --> 00:17:44.930
are in the system already to feel confident
they can come and have their appointments

00:17:44.930 --> 00:17:49.800
and come into hospitals but also to make sure
that we're really talking to local communities

00:17:49.800 --> 00:17:52.360
to give them confidence to seek help if they
need it.

00:17:52.360 --> 00:17:57.270
&gt;&gt;Wol&nbsp;Kolade: Thank you.
&gt;&gt;David&nbsp;Prior: Thank you. Any other questions

00:17:57.270 --> 00:18:01.420
for Matt or Amanda?
Well, Amanda, can we go straight on to the

00:18:01.420 --> 00:18:07.060
next item, which is yours as well, please?
&gt;&gt;Amanda&nbsp;Pritchard: Thank you David. So,

00:18:07.060 --> 00:18:12.860
this – I referred to this a moment ago but
the next paper is about our approach to recovery,

00:18:12.860 --> 00:18:19.460
so we have sent – myself and Simon – two&nbsp;letters
in the past. One was really asking the NHS

00:18:19.460 --> 00:18:24.280
to prepare for the first wave of COVID; the
second was talking about the importance of

00:18:24.280 --> 00:18:30.900
restoration of critical services. The next
communication that we've been developing – and

00:18:30.900 --> 00:18:35.940
developing, I should say, very closely in
conjunction with both colleagues across NHS&nbsp;England

00:18:35.940 --> 00:18:42.990
and NHS&nbsp;Improvement but also with quite deep
engagement with colleagues across the NHS

00:18:42.990 --> 00:18:49.950
and support from other colleagues in government
and more widely – has been our sort of phase‑three

00:18:49.950 --> 00:18:53.570
communication, which is really about the next
stage of our recovery.

00:18:53.570 --> 00:19:00.790
So what we're intending to do is get something
out, potentially even this week if we can,

00:19:00.790 --> 00:19:05.190
which gives people a sense of the priorities
going forward. So, at the moment, what we're

00:19:05.190 --> 00:19:09.940
describing is a number of key priorities,
if I just say very quickly what they are.

00:19:09.940 --> 00:19:15.630
So, building on what I've just said, absolutely
this focus now on recovering as much elective

00:19:15.630 --> 00:19:20.490
activity as possible, so that's not just the
urgent work, it's the routine activity, as

00:19:20.490 --> 00:19:25.540
well, getting back as near as possible to
pre‑COVID levels of activity, that's across

00:19:25.540 --> 00:19:33.130
outpatients, diagnostics, day case inpatient
care, recognising that it's not straightforward

00:19:33.130 --> 00:19:37.290
because we have to do that in a way that's
safe for our patients, safe for our staff

00:19:37.290 --> 00:19:42.800
but also recognising the reality, which is,
thankfully, levels of COVID are currently

00:19:42.800 --> 00:19:49.110
low and therefore we do have a window now,
certainly, where we can prioritise getting

00:19:49.110 --> 00:19:54.720
back to normal as much as possible and we
need to do that in order to make sure that

00:19:54.720 --> 00:19:58.660
we're delivering the care our patients need,
so that's a big priority in the letter, also

00:19:58.660 --> 00:20:02.750
talking about – and again, it links back
to something Simon said in his introduction

00:20:02.750 --> 00:20:06.730
– about the importance of health inequalities
being at the heart of our approach to recovery,

00:20:06.730 --> 00:20:11.809
so encouraging people to continue to work
in systems, to continue to work with partners

00:20:11.809 --> 00:20:17.680
and make sure that we're really thinking about
how we provide care to a population, rather

00:20:17.680 --> 00:20:20.460
than just thinking about a sort of organisational‑level
approach.

00:20:20.460 --> 00:20:27.420
Other headlines – you would expect me to
say this but restoring primary care and community

00:20:27.420 --> 00:20:32.300
services, because that is the bedrock from
which so much else in our service is built.

00:20:32.300 --> 00:20:37.440
Really good news that we've got funding – continued
funding from the government for care for the

00:20:37.440 --> 00:20:41.960
first six&nbsp;weeks following discharge, so that's
an important component of the community offer.

00:20:41.960 --> 00:20:45.770
We're talking about mental health services
in the letter, in line with what I've just

00:20:45.770 --> 00:20:50.500
said about what we now expect to see and are
seeing in terms of that growth in demand for

00:20:50.500 --> 00:20:55.140
mental health services and of course we're
continuing to talk about the importance of

00:20:55.140 --> 00:20:58.080
learning disabilities, autism services specifically,
within the letter.

00:20:58.080 --> 00:21:02.510
Other things we're talking about – again,
you'd expect me to say this but it is about

00:21:02.510 --> 00:21:10.441
our cancer care and making sure in that that
we are prioritising access for patients who

00:21:10.441 --> 00:21:14.230
are on cancer pathways.
The final thing that we're really trying to

00:21:14.230 --> 00:21:19.890
kind of call out in the letter is the look‑forward
to winter. So, what does that mean? Well,

00:21:19.890 --> 00:21:26.559
very specifically, it means an unprecedented
scale of flu vaccination campaign. So that

00:21:26.559 --> 00:21:32.790
is both for the population but of course also
for our own people and that's going to be

00:21:32.790 --> 00:21:37.540
an important feature of our preparations for
winter, continuing, of course, also to make

00:21:37.540 --> 00:21:43.680
sure we're well set up to respond to any local
outbreaks, should they arise and that we're

00:21:43.680 --> 00:21:48.510
continuing absolutely to have that appropriate
focus on infection control, which includes

00:21:48.510 --> 00:21:55.720
things like staff testing and the ability
to make sure that we're able to respond should

00:21:55.720 --> 00:22:00.240
there be a second wave more generally as well.
The final thing, I think, probably for me

00:22:00.240 --> 00:22:05.180
to say – and it links to, again, some of
the other conversation we've had – is that

00:22:05.180 --> 00:22:11.200
we're really wanting also to encourage colleagues
across the NHS, where we have been able to

00:22:11.200 --> 00:22:15.460
make beneficial changes in this first wave
response to COVID, so where we've been able

00:22:15.460 --> 00:22:22.450
to do things like use 111 to help direct people
to urgent care services that aren't a front‑door,

00:22:22.450 --> 00:22:27.430
physical presentation at A&amp;E, where we are
able to use digital alternatives to face‑to‑face

00:22:27.430 --> 00:22:31.790
care, really trying to make sure that in our
letter, we encourage people to continue to

00:22:31.790 --> 00:22:37.680
do that and really lock in that way of working
to become something that's much more routine

00:22:37.680 --> 00:22:40.930
in future.
&gt;&gt;David&nbsp;Prior: Amanda, thank you very much

00:22:40.930 --> 00:22:46.440
for that. You couldn't just elaborate a little
bit on the diagnostic hubs, could you? I'm

00:22:46.440 --> 00:22:50.701
slightly – I'm not quite sure what they
mean and how far you've got with it.

00:22:50.701 --> 00:22:55.021
&gt;&gt;Amanda&nbsp;Pritchard: Diagnostic – so the
thing that I was referring to earlier was

00:22:55.021 --> 00:23:00.480
the cancer hubs. So that's been really – that's
been the cancer alliances that have organised

00:23:00.480 --> 00:23:06.730
hubs in each of the 21&nbsp;alliances to make
sure, really, through the first wave of the

00:23:06.730 --> 00:23:13.870
pandemic but they've continued on with this,
that they had a, sort of, if you like, COVID‑minimal,

00:23:13.870 --> 00:23:19.450
COVID‑safe way of continuing to provide,
in particular, surgical treatment for cancer

00:23:19.450 --> 00:23:23.770
patients. Often that meant using the independent
sector of course, because they were able to

00:23:23.770 --> 00:23:30.900
do that as a way of protecting cancer pathways
from routine access through other hospitals.

00:23:30.900 --> 00:23:35.490
For diagnostic hubs, that is very much part
of – it's part of the long‑term planning

00:23:35.490 --> 00:23:38.580
commitment, actually around where we were
going with diagnostics and it recognises,

00:23:38.580 --> 00:23:44.950
I think, the work that Mike&nbsp;Richards has
led on looking at where there is an under‑provision

00:23:44.950 --> 00:23:50.540
of access to the diagnostics and a desire,
of course, to invest in improving that. What

00:23:50.540 --> 00:23:57.450
we're doing at the moment is trying to use
a number of different approaches. One is this

00:23:57.450 --> 00:24:04.690
process that we've called 'adapt and adopt'
to bring a really creative, clinically‑led

00:24:04.690 --> 00:24:09.610
kind of approach to thinking about how we
restore services, how we expand services and

00:24:09.610 --> 00:24:15.290
diagnostics has been very much a part of that
and one of the things that they have done

00:24:15.290 --> 00:24:21.670
is come up with ideas such as using the Nightingales,
where they are physically set up to do this

00:24:21.670 --> 00:24:27.780
well, to provide diagnostic services while
they're not needed for coronavirus care. So

00:24:27.780 --> 00:24:36.460
the Exeter Nightingale and the Harrogate&nbsp;Nightingale,
or the North‑Eastern&nbsp;Yorkshire Nightingale,

00:24:36.460 --> 00:24:41.280
which is based in Harrogate, both of them
are currently operating effectively as diagnostic

00:24:41.280 --> 00:24:45.790
hubs and it's that sort of creative approach
that we're encouraging in the short term.

00:24:45.790 --> 00:24:49.950
But it is absolutely part of the long‑term
investment and the long‑term strategic direction

00:24:49.950 --> 00:24:53.750
for community diagnostics.
&gt;&gt;David&nbsp;Prior: Okay, good. Well, thank you

00:24:53.750 --> 00:24:58.530
very much Amanda. I'm sorry, this is quite
a quick sort of run‑through. As you know,

00:24:58.530 --> 00:25:04.429
this was a board meeting that was put in – David&nbsp;Roberts,
you've got a question.

00:25:04.429 --> 00:25:09.820
&gt;&gt;David&nbsp;Roberts: Thank you. Amanda, one of
the things that we've been bedevilled with

00:25:09.820 --> 00:25:16.380
over a number of years is the variation in
outcomes and quality and cost that we see

00:25:16.380 --> 00:25:24.080
across different parts of the health system.
Given the need for improved productivity across

00:25:24.080 --> 00:25:31.929
the piece, to what extent are we able to use
this as an opportunity to try and reduce variation

00:25:31.929 --> 00:25:36.420
and share learnings and best practice from
across the country to be able to improve outcomes

00:25:36.420 --> 00:25:42.059
for patients and productivity and therefore
for more patients, not just for those who

00:25:42.059 --> 00:25:46.900
we've been able to serve?
&gt;&gt;Amanda&nbsp;Pritchard: Thank you David. So,

00:25:46.900 --> 00:25:53.130
I think you're absolutely right to pick this
out as, again, one of those areas where I

00:25:53.130 --> 00:25:57.170
think there is – you know, it's been such
a hard experience, hasn't it, the last few

00:25:57.170 --> 00:26:03.790
months for COVID, but looking for those opportunities
and those things that we have done well that

00:26:03.790 --> 00:26:09.140
we can build on and do more of. I would say
one of the very obvious things has been around

00:26:09.140 --> 00:26:14.790
the strength of clinical leadership through
these first few months and what we are doing

00:26:14.790 --> 00:26:20.060
at the moment, with the leadership of Tim&nbsp;Briggs,
who leads the GIRFT programme – Getting&nbsp;It&nbsp;Right&nbsp;First&nbsp;Time

00:26:20.060 --> 00:26:27.380
– and the regions, in particular London,
who are kind of pioneering this work, is saying,

00:26:27.380 --> 00:26:33.070
'Well, as we restore services, let's use the
clinical leadership and the expertise that

00:26:33.070 --> 00:26:40.090
sits in our existing GIRFT programme and apply
that across the whole of the approach to restoration.

00:26:40.090 --> 00:26:45.750
So they've been very targeted in which areas:
they've started with orthopaedics in particular

00:26:45.750 --> 00:26:51.020
and ophthalmology but will intend to spread
not just now out across the rest of the country

00:26:51.020 --> 00:26:58.080
but also of course to a wider number of services,
as they begin to plan and implement that restoration

00:26:58.080 --> 00:27:01.560
activity.
So, absolutely David and I think this is a

00:27:01.560 --> 00:27:07.240
real opportunity for us to fast‑track something
that, again, we would want to do anyway but

00:27:07.240 --> 00:27:11.600
really using our kind of clinical colleagues
and clinical leaders to help drive that.

00:27:11.600 --> 00:27:16.710
&gt;&gt;David&nbsp;Prior: Thank you Amanda. Ruth?
&gt;&gt;Ruth&nbsp;May: Thank you very much, Chair. So,

00:27:16.710 --> 00:27:20.631
in support of all of what Amanda has done
is none of this can be achieved unless we've

00:27:20.631 --> 00:27:26.560
got our people that can support the delivery
of this, so, from a nursing point of view,

00:27:26.560 --> 00:27:31.500
I'm delighted that we've got over 12,000&nbsp;more
registered nurses now in providers than we

00:27:31.500 --> 00:27:38.230
did have this time last year. Our vacancy
rate has gone down from 43,000 to 36,000 but

00:27:38.230 --> 00:27:42.730
there's still more to do and that's why I'm
committed in supporting Amanda and Julian's

00:27:42.730 --> 00:27:49.309
work and the board's work on this that we
really focus on three&nbsp;buckets of work to

00:27:49.309 --> 00:27:55.410
support the need to achieve more registered
nurses very shortly. Our domestic supply – I'm

00:27:55.410 --> 00:28:01.080
really pleased to see the 20% increase of
UCAS applications and the work that we're

00:28:01.080 --> 00:28:06.630
doing jointly across HEE and ourselves to
attract more people to want to be a nurse.

00:28:06.630 --> 00:28:11.690
And of course, the retention work, retaining
our people, that Prerana and Emily are leading

00:28:11.690 --> 00:28:16.220
is fantastic and of course, then the work
of the international recruitment. It's obviously

00:28:16.220 --> 00:28:21.970
been slightly delayed given that travel restrictions
have been in place during COVID but we have

00:28:21.970 --> 00:28:26.700
now at least started to see some of those
come through. We had 23&nbsp;nurses come from

00:28:26.700 --> 00:28:31.820
India, the week before last, that are going
off to Sheffield, so we're starting to see

00:28:31.820 --> 00:28:37.360
some of that work and I'm sure Duncan and
myself will continue to work on that. So collectively,

00:28:37.360 --> 00:28:43.150
we support the deliverability of what Amanda
has just been talking about.

00:28:43.150 --> 00:28:47.940
&gt;&gt;David&nbsp;Prior: Good, thank you very much,
Ruth. Good, well thank you very much for that,

00:28:47.940 --> 00:28:52.770
Amanda, that was really helpful and informative.
So, the next item is around the future of

00:28:52.770 --> 00:28:59.710
urgent and emergency care services and I think
Steve and Pauline, you're going to lead this

00:28:59.710 --> 00:29:00.710
discussion.
&gt;&gt;Steve&nbsp;Powis: Yes, thank you, Chair and

00:29:00.710 --> 00:29:04.670
in fact I'm going to immediately hand over
to Pauline, who leads this work. Pauline?

00:29:04.670 --> 00:29:10.309
&gt;&gt;Pauline&nbsp;Philip: Thanks Steve. So, the paper
that you have in front of you essentially

00:29:10.309 --> 00:29:17.590
covers two areas, as far as urgent and emergency
care is concerned. Some of it addresses the

00:29:17.590 --> 00:29:24.860
preparation that we're doing for winter and
importantly, it also focuses on the next phase

00:29:24.860 --> 00:29:33.080
of transformation. And I think board members
will be very much aware that over recent years,

00:29:33.080 --> 00:29:38.799
from a UEC perspective, we've been driving
ahead with this transformation agenda. At

00:29:38.799 --> 00:29:47.100
the heart of the agenda is an ambition to
ensure that, if people need urgent care, that

00:29:47.100 --> 00:29:53.380
they can receive it locally and receive it
in the best possible place. But if they are

00:29:53.380 --> 00:29:58.990
seriously ill, if they're in life‑threatening
situations, that they will get to the right

00:29:58.990 --> 00:30:04.510
place, have the right level of expertise in
the right time.

00:30:04.510 --> 00:30:10.880
Clearly that ambition is very much enabled
by the development of new models of care,

00:30:10.880 --> 00:30:16.809
technology, etcetera.
The last couple of months have obviously been

00:30:16.809 --> 00:30:23.970
a very interesting and somewhat challenging
period, from an urgent care perspective but

00:30:23.970 --> 00:30:31.030
we have also seen some significant changes.
We've seen changes from a patient point of

00:30:31.030 --> 00:30:37.390
view, i.e. how our patients want to access
services and their expectations of how those

00:30:37.390 --> 00:30:45.070
services should be delivered. We have also
seen service changes, in some ways, being

00:30:45.070 --> 00:30:51.780
fast‑tracked, changes that we had been planning
over a period of time are suddenly being delivered

00:30:51.780 --> 00:30:55.450
overnight.
So, what we've set out in this paper are sort

00:30:55.450 --> 00:31:00.710
of five&nbsp;pillars moving forward. I'm not going
to talk to each of them in detail but just

00:31:00.710 --> 00:31:07.260
highlight a couple. I think the first one
is building capacity for this winter and beyond

00:31:07.260 --> 00:31:15.560
that, an investment of £450&nbsp;million, which
we will be announcing in detail over the coming

00:31:15.560 --> 00:31:22.679
days. And the purpose of that money is to
ensure that our urgent and emergency care

00:31:22.679 --> 00:31:27.590
services, our department, the front of our
hospitals are safe from a communal[?&nbsp;00.31.27]

00:31:27.590 --> 00:31:35.360
point of view but also to enable some of the
transformation that will take place now and

00:31:35.360 --> 00:31:41.000
in the immediate future.
The second area that the paper addresses is

00:31:41.000 --> 00:31:49.390
focusing on 111 services. I think in Amanda's
earlier report to you and in previous reports

00:31:49.390 --> 00:31:55.850
to the board we've talked a lot about the
111 service and what it successfully delivered

00:31:55.850 --> 00:32:02.370
during the COVID period and we see, even now,
that we have more calls, we've got more people

00:32:02.370 --> 00:32:09.940
going online. It's a service that our population
has very much valued and what we want to do

00:32:09.940 --> 00:32:16.919
is to be able to further develop that service
and to really talk about 111 first, so that

00:32:16.919 --> 00:32:22.170
we can encourage people that if they feel
they have an urgent or emergency care need,

00:32:22.170 --> 00:32:29.420
that they go online to 111 or they pick up
the phone and contact 111 before they do anything

00:32:29.420 --> 00:32:36.340
else. And that by so doing, that they will
get a better offer and that it will be easier

00:32:36.340 --> 00:32:42.290
for them to be directed to the service that
best meets their needs.

00:32:42.290 --> 00:32:47.841
Clearly, in order to do that, a lot of work
is taking place behind the scenes; we're increasing

00:32:47.841 --> 00:32:53.760
the clinical advisory service behind 111,
we're increasing the amount of call handlers,

00:32:53.760 --> 00:32:59.230
increasing, really importantly, the directory
of services that 111 can access, so that they

00:32:59.230 --> 00:33:07.170
have very real places to direct people when
they present. For the first time, introducing

00:33:07.170 --> 00:33:13.630
direct booking into our emergency departments.
So if it is felt that the best thing to meet

00:33:13.630 --> 00:33:18.809
your need is to actually go to an emergency
department, instead of having to go along

00:33:18.809 --> 00:33:25.880
and queue up, you can be given a booked appointment,
etcetera, so you will be able to have a better

00:33:25.880 --> 00:33:32.179
experience of arriving and being seen almost
straight away and importantly, communicating

00:33:32.179 --> 00:33:36.940
all of these changes.
So, that's the sort of – the secondary and

00:33:36.940 --> 00:33:43.100
it has to be supported by the development
of new pathways and technology, etcetera.

00:33:43.100 --> 00:33:49.860
We've got a number of early movers, one really
in each region that is trying out this different

00:33:49.860 --> 00:33:56.820
way of delivering 111, or this enhanced way
of delivering 111 and all of the feedback

00:33:56.820 --> 00:34:02.710
that we are getting from those first‑movers
is really positive. In fact, I think right

00:34:02.710 --> 00:34:08.260
across the NHS, people want to move ahead
with this as quickly as possible and certainly

00:34:08.260 --> 00:34:14.659
in advance of the winter period.
Just to mention the critical role that 999

00:34:14.659 --> 00:34:20.860
will also play, as far as this is concerned
and going back again to Amanda's report, I

00:34:20.860 --> 00:34:27.889
just want to draw out the fact that, for the
second month in a row, our 999 services have

00:34:27.889 --> 00:34:33.549
been able to meet all six&nbsp;standards and I
think it's fair to say that the ambulance

00:34:33.549 --> 00:34:39.639
service has gone from strength to strength
in the last six&nbsp;months but, really importantly,

00:34:39.639 --> 00:34:45.879
they've continued the transformation agenda,
having more see and treat, hear and treat

00:34:45.879 --> 00:34:50.490
types of services, so it's not always conveyancing
to hospital.

00:34:50.490 --> 00:34:56.210
Clearly, over the course of the coming weeks
and months, what we're really doing is sort

00:34:56.210 --> 00:35:02.910
of introducing something like a different
social norm: that people traditionally have

00:35:02.910 --> 00:35:07.739
accessed emergency care by turning up in an
emergency department and now we're saying

00:35:07.739 --> 00:35:15.200
there are better ways of doing this and having
a comms campaign that will really enable people

00:35:15.200 --> 00:35:19.370
to use the service most effectively.
Just a couple of other quick things and then

00:35:19.370 --> 00:35:27.289
I will stop. In order for our urgent and emergency
care services to deliver and successfully

00:35:27.289 --> 00:35:33.280
in the winter period, we, as always, are very
much aware of the need to ensure that our

00:35:33.280 --> 00:35:41.960
hospital capacity is there and this year it's
probably a bigger challenge than in previous

00:35:41.960 --> 00:35:47.789
years from an infection control point of view,
etcetera, so making sure that we've got the

00:35:47.789 --> 00:35:53.049
right number of beds and continuing the transformation
work that we have been doing around length

00:35:53.049 --> 00:36:01.559
of stay, working with our external partners
around discharge. We have seen some excellent

00:36:01.559 --> 00:36:07.779
results in the last couple of months, etcetera,
around how we can really try and ensure that

00:36:07.779 --> 00:36:13.680
when it's right for a patient to leave hospital,
that they are able to return to their home

00:36:13.680 --> 00:36:18.140
and to their place of residence as soon as
possible and being able to continue to do

00:36:18.140 --> 00:36:22.589
that.
And finally, the last thing that I just wanted

00:36:22.589 --> 00:36:28.349
to mention – and I'm going to really hand
back to Steve – is how all of this connects

00:36:28.349 --> 00:36:33.640
in with the review we've been doing about
how we measure the performance of our services

00:36:33.640 --> 00:36:37.549
and our clinical services review. So, I'll
hand back to Steve.

00:36:37.549 --> 00:36:41.740
&gt;&gt;Steve&nbsp;Powis: Thanks Pauline. So, I think
the last important thing to say is obviously

00:36:41.740 --> 00:36:46.119
how we measure performance is really critical
too and as the board is aware, we have been

00:36:46.119 --> 00:36:53.349
undertaking, at the request of the former
prime minister, a review of the clinical standards

00:36:53.349 --> 00:36:59.109
that we use throughout the NHS, including
in urgent and emergency care. That work had

00:36:59.109 --> 00:37:07.470
progressed last year with pilots in 14&nbsp;sites
across the country, who were helping us develop

00:37:07.470 --> 00:37:12.960
a set of metrics that we initially proposed
just over a year ago but we have worked on

00:37:12.960 --> 00:37:18.980
with those pilot sites. Prior to COVID, we
were working very closely with patient groups,

00:37:18.980 --> 00:37:24.910
with important stakeholders, such as the Royal&nbsp;Colleges,
to develop those initial set of metrics, with

00:37:24.910 --> 00:37:33.489
the input from the pilot sites into an advanced
set of measures and we, I think – well,

00:37:33.489 --> 00:37:38.980
I know we had agreed on a bundle of metrics
that we felt were very pertinent to the way

00:37:38.980 --> 00:37:46.619
we wanted to reform urgent and emergency care.
Our view is that COVID has cemented the view

00:37:46.619 --> 00:37:53.829
that we collectively had before the pandemic,
that these were the right metrics and that

00:37:53.829 --> 00:38:00.500
they are important metrics to look at going
forward and so we are now in a position that

00:38:00.500 --> 00:38:07.589
we hope to publish a report soon and are of
course in discussion with colleagues in the

00:38:07.589 --> 00:38:12.839
Department&nbsp;of&nbsp;Health and elsewhere in government
as to that report.

00:38:12.839 --> 00:38:20.859
&gt;&gt;David&nbsp;Prior: Thank you very much, Steve.
Any questions for Pauline or Steve? This is

00:38:20.859 --> 00:38:25.220
quite a profound change, isn't it, Steve,
you know, this could make a huge difference

00:38:25.220 --> 00:38:29.010
to, sort of, A&amp;E performance and the treatment
of people in urgent care[?&nbsp;00.38.29].

00:38:29.010 --> 00:38:32.819
&gt;&gt;Steve&nbsp;Powis: So I think what's important,
as Pauline has highlighted, is that this is

00:38:32.819 --> 00:38:40.450
a series of changes. This is a transformational
project, of which measurement is one part

00:38:40.450 --> 00:38:48.019
but actually the key parts are doing things
differently in the way that we ensure that

00:38:48.019 --> 00:38:54.200
our processes to access emergency care, ensure
that patients are treated in the right place

00:38:54.200 --> 00:38:57.869
at the right time and that's fundamentally
what this is about.

00:38:57.869 --> 00:39:02.130
&gt;&gt;David&nbsp;Prior: Good. Thank you very much
and Pauline, thank you very much for all the

00:39:02.130 --> 00:39:07.769
work that you've done on this as well.
The next item is clinical innovations during

00:39:07.769 --> 00:39:12.099
the last sort of three or four&nbsp;months and
that's you, Steve and is Kathy joining the

00:39:12.099 --> 00:39:14.609
call as well, or not [inaudible&nbsp;00.39.13]?
&gt;&gt;Steve&nbsp;Powis: No, Kathy is on annual leave,

00:39:14.609 --> 00:39:20.559
so it's just me, so thank you, Chair. So,
I think we have touched on this subject a

00:39:20.559 --> 00:39:26.729
number of times in the last few items because
both Amanda and Pauline have highlighted some

00:39:26.729 --> 00:39:34.309
of the innovations and change that has been
required during the pandemic and a lot of

00:39:34.309 --> 00:39:39.119
that, as I think Amanda highlighted, is work
that we already wanted to do as part of the

00:39:39.119 --> 00:39:46.630
long‑term plan but in fact we've had to
accelerate and go harder and faster as a result

00:39:46.630 --> 00:39:52.420
of the pandemic and I think the first thing
I'd say is I think, as Simon has said previously,

00:39:52.420 --> 00:39:59.229
this has really shown how agile and flexible
the NHS can be when it is absolutely required

00:39:59.229 --> 00:40:04.130
to be.
Of course, of those many, many innovations,

00:40:04.130 --> 00:40:10.469
we want to identify and embed those innovations
that we believe will improve patient care

00:40:10.469 --> 00:40:16.069
and this paper really sets out for the board
how we are going about identifying those innovations

00:40:16.069 --> 00:40:23.319
and ensuring that we capture them and we can
embed them where appropriate.

00:40:23.319 --> 00:40:26.910
I'm not going to repeat the paper but it does
highlight some of the key innovations and

00:40:26.910 --> 00:40:31.849
you've heard some of those already. We've
just talked about changes to 111 and the emergency

00:40:31.849 --> 00:40:39.430
care pathway. Amanda highlighted how we have
digitised in remote consultations a lot faster.

00:40:39.430 --> 00:40:47.150
So, one statistic for you is that in January&nbsp;2020,
earlier this year, I think 30% of practices

00:40:47.150 --> 00:40:52.520
were covered by the deployment of remote online
consultation and video consultation and by

00:40:52.520 --> 00:40:58.359
June that was 90% of general practices, so
a rapid expansion over a very short period

00:40:58.359 --> 00:41:03.630
of time. And of course, although it's entirely
appropriate for patients still to be seen

00:41:03.630 --> 00:41:10.650
face to face, where that's important, we and
general practice want to hold on to the advances

00:41:10.650 --> 00:41:13.329
that we've made there.
And the other example I would give you is

00:41:13.329 --> 00:41:19.049
on clinical trials because this is an international
example and the NHS has managed to put a huge

00:41:19.049 --> 00:41:23.979
number, more than 130,000&nbsp;patients into the
recovery trials, which I think has shown how

00:41:23.979 --> 00:41:31.140
you can rapidly roll out – develop and roll
out trial protocols during an emergency, a

00:41:31.140 --> 00:41:38.339
health emergency and rapidly get clinical
results that have been peer reviewed, published

00:41:38.339 --> 00:41:42.849
and are no doubt changing the course of the
management of the pandemic and saving lives,

00:41:42.849 --> 00:41:49.319
not just in the UK but throughout the world.
So, we said about capturing those innovations,

00:41:49.319 --> 00:41:54.920
led by the improvement directorate under Hugh&nbsp;McCaughey.
We called for submissions from frontline clinicians

00:41:54.920 --> 00:41:59.670
and professionals to capture all the innovations
that have occurred. We've had over 1,000&nbsp;responses

00:41:59.670 --> 00:42:08.691
and we are now in the process of analysing
those, led by Hugh's directorate and as I

00:42:08.691 --> 00:42:13.990
said at the start, working out what we need
to hold on to, what we need to embed and how

00:42:13.990 --> 00:42:18.369
we do that going forward.
&gt;&gt;David&nbsp;Prior: Thank you very much, Steve.

00:42:18.369 --> 00:42:23.530
Joanne, you'd like to come in, I think.
&gt;&gt;Joanna&nbsp;Shaw: Yes, thank you to – I mean

00:42:23.530 --> 00:42:28.380
it is a question about innovation and it – actually
it relates, in a sense, more to the previous

00:42:28.380 --> 00:42:34.640
paper. It's about the way that 111 is interacting
with the rest of the NHS. So, I remember that

00:42:34.640 --> 00:42:42.739
when 111 was introduced, one of its real advantages
was going to be that, because the technology

00:42:42.739 --> 00:42:45.980
that underpinned it and the software that
underpinned it was kind of home‑grown within

00:42:45.980 --> 00:42:53.200
the NHS, that GPs would trust it enough to
allow 111 – people who worked in 111 to

00:42:53.200 --> 00:42:59.150
book patients in directly for appointments
to their GP. So, in other words, if somebody

00:42:59.150 --> 00:43:06.309
rang up 111 and 111 decided they needed to
see a GP face to face, that the patient didn't

00:43:06.309 --> 00:43:10.489
have to start again with their GP but 111
could book them straight in.

00:43:10.489 --> 00:43:16.140
Now that we're using 111 in this sort of new,
innovative way, as a front door to urgent

00:43:16.140 --> 00:43:23.049
care, what I wanted to ask was has that vision,
that was originally there for 111, has it

00:43:23.049 --> 00:43:29.680
been realised and how – how are – how
successful have you been to persuade GPs that

00:43:29.680 --> 00:43:35.540
– so GPs naturally feel that they know their
patients best and they're the best judges

00:43:35.540 --> 00:43:39.250
of whether the patient needs to meet face
to face, so you know, have we got to the point

00:43:39.250 --> 00:43:46.160
where 111 can book people directly into their
GP, where 111 feels that they need to see

00:43:46.160 --> 00:43:49.609
a GP face to face?
&gt;&gt;David&nbsp;Prior: So I think Pauline might have

00:43:49.609 --> 00:43:51.829
left.
&gt;&gt;Pauline&nbsp;Philip: No, I'm here.

00:43:51.829 --> 00:43:55.609
&gt;&gt;David&nbsp;Prior: You're there, sorry, I just
couldn't see you.

00:43:55.609 --> 00:44:00.540
&gt;&gt;Pauline&nbsp;Philip: So I think that's a really,
really good question and one of the things

00:44:00.540 --> 00:44:06.690
that I probably should have said in my report,
that I didn't, is that, in developing 111

00:44:06.690 --> 00:44:12.930
first, in each locality, in each area, it's
fundamentally important that we work with

00:44:12.930 --> 00:44:18.950
general practice as well and in the local
messaging and indeed in our regional and national

00:44:18.950 --> 00:44:26.329
messaging, that what we are actually saying
to people is, 'Look, if you have these types

00:44:26.329 --> 00:44:33.910
of issues, please contact your GP first. It
is only if you have an urgent need that means

00:44:33.910 --> 00:44:40.160
that you cannot wait until you can next see
your GP, that you really should be contacting

00:44:40.160 --> 00:44:47.039
111, or going online.' But I think in answer
to the question about where we can book patients

00:44:47.039 --> 00:44:53.460
from 111, yes, we can book patients in to
some of our GP slots and that's been very

00:44:53.460 --> 00:44:59.460
successful and now we will be able to book
patients in to our emergency departments as

00:44:59.460 --> 00:45:04.010
well.
&gt;&gt;David&nbsp;Prior: Good, thank you. Thank you

00:45:04.010 --> 00:45:09.099
very much, Pauline. Wol?
&gt;&gt;Wol&nbsp;Kolade: So, one of the things that's

00:45:09.099 --> 00:45:14.200
happened through this process of the pandemic
is, as you say, there's lots of innovation

00:45:14.200 --> 00:45:18.670
coming through but the way the paper is written,
it sort of seems as if it's all come from

00:45:18.670 --> 00:45:21.969
the NHS itself, when in fact it's probably
a bit more of a collaboration with the private

00:45:21.969 --> 00:45:26.119
sector and involvement with the private sector
and I'm sure some of the hundreds and I think

00:45:26.119 --> 00:45:31.049
maybe even thousands of responses you've got
will involve collaborations with the private

00:45:31.049 --> 00:45:36.690
sector. The impression the NHS has given in
the past years is being a very difficult,

00:45:36.690 --> 00:45:41.140
monolithic place to introduce innovation into
and yet we have shown we can be very porous,

00:45:41.140 --> 00:45:46.219
if we like; how do we make sure we capture
that and encourage that, going forward?

00:45:46.219 --> 00:45:51.599
&gt;&gt;Steve&nbsp;Powis: Well, I think you're right,
Wol and of course innovation comes from many

00:45:51.599 --> 00:45:56.900
places and it won't just be the private sector,
of course; innovation has come from other

00:45:56.900 --> 00:46:03.099
parts of the wider health and social care
system, so clearly the National&nbsp;Institute&nbsp;for&nbsp;Health&nbsp;Research

00:46:03.099 --> 00:46:09.740
and research partners have worked very closely
on – and have driven, with us, the recruitment

00:46:09.740 --> 00:46:16.880
of people into trials, so I wouldn't want
to – well, I certainly want to ensure that

00:46:16.880 --> 00:46:22.460
it's not just the NHS that has driven innovation.
I think, on the question as to how we capture

00:46:22.460 --> 00:46:29.119
that and ensure that, going forward, we don't
lose the momentum, I think Amanda talked a

00:46:29.119 --> 00:46:34.749
little bit about it earlier and we have set
our regions challenges to do that and they

00:46:34.749 --> 00:46:40.170
are – each region is working on a set of
specific innovation challenges going forward.

00:46:40.170 --> 00:46:44.580
We have our academic health science networks
already there and one of the things that they

00:46:44.580 --> 00:46:52.559
do is drive innovation and only this morning
Hugh's team was leading a workshop with our

00:46:52.559 --> 00:46:58.650
AHSNs on exactly this topic.
So, I think it's both a combination of ensuring

00:46:58.650 --> 00:47:05.140
that we are using these structure, those partnerships,
those processes we've already got to drive

00:47:05.140 --> 00:47:11.489
innovation but driving harder and faster and
no doubt capturing, actually through the innovations

00:47:11.489 --> 00:47:13.989
programme, new ways of innovating and new
ways of going forward.

00:47:13.989 --> 00:47:19.410
&gt;&gt;David&nbsp;Prior: Yeah, well I think it's the
critical question, actually: how do we ensure

00:47:19.410 --> 00:47:25.650
that the NHS remains as open to innovation
and accelerates the adoption of the innovation

00:47:25.650 --> 00:47:30.309
as we go forward? It's absolutely crucial,
so a really big question, I think.

00:47:30.309 --> 00:47:35.170
So, thank you for that. We're now moving on
to the situation at Shrewsbury and Telford

00:47:35.170 --> 00:47:39.599
and Dido – are you there, Dido?
&gt;&gt;Dido&nbsp;Harding: Yes, yes, I'm here.

00:47:39.599 --> 00:47:45.489
&gt;&gt;David&nbsp;Prior: Dido, over to you, please.
&gt;&gt;Dido&nbsp;Harding: Right, of course, David.

00:47:45.489 --> 00:47:52.660
So, I think the first thing to say is that
I just – we'd ask the board to take note

00:47:52.660 --> 00:48:00.930
of the update in the papers on Shrewsbury&nbsp;and&nbsp;Telford&nbsp;Hospital,
specifically both the independent maternity

00:48:00.930 --> 00:48:08.760
review and the wider action that's being taken
by the CQC and NHS&nbsp;Improvement in response

00:48:08.760 --> 00:48:17.380
to quite a range of quality concerns. And
I chaired a board to system review meeting

00:48:17.380 --> 00:48:22.142
on Friday, attended by Amanda; Aidan&nbsp;Fowler,
our Patient&nbsp;Safety&nbsp;Director; Julian&nbsp;Kelly,

00:48:22.142 --> 00:48:30.130
our Chief&nbsp;Financial&nbsp;Officer; Ruth; Dale&nbsp;Bywater,
the Regional&nbsp;Director and then a number of

00:48:30.130 --> 00:48:35.940
leaders from the trust, from the board and
from the local system. And I think we all

00:48:35.940 --> 00:48:45.200
came away from that, that meeting, very concerned
about the issues there and the need really

00:48:45.200 --> 00:48:51.940
to take quite significant action, both locally
in the individual organisation, in the trust,

00:48:51.940 --> 00:48:57.660
across the system and also for us, as NHS&nbsp;Improvement,
to make sure that we are providing both the

00:48:57.660 --> 00:49:03.240
right support and the right challenge to address,
as I say, the really quite significant issues.

00:49:03.240 --> 00:49:07.099
And I wonder whether, Amanda, you would just
like to comment first and then hand over to

00:49:07.099 --> 00:49:13.269
Steve to comment on the Ockenden&nbsp;Review specifically?
&gt;&gt;Amanda&nbsp;Pritchard: Yes, Dido, thank you

00:49:13.269 --> 00:49:19.920
very much. So, I think the first thing to
say – and I know I speak for all my colleagues,

00:49:19.920 --> 00:49:25.229
when I say this, is that I think we would
want to start by offering sincere sympathies

00:49:25.229 --> 00:49:33.719
to all family members, patients who've been
affected by any failures in care at the trust.

00:49:33.719 --> 00:49:39.609
Shrewsbury&nbsp;and&nbsp;Telford, as colleagues know,
has been challenged for some period of time.

00:49:39.609 --> 00:49:46.859
It is not – this is not an organisation
where this is a new set of concerns but clearly,

00:49:46.859 --> 00:49:51.880
when I received a letter from Ted&nbsp;Baker,
who's the Chief&nbsp;Inspector of Hospitals, from

00:49:51.880 --> 00:49:59.369
the CQC outlining some really quite significant
concerns recently, we took the opportunity

00:49:59.369 --> 00:50:05.479
not just to meet with him but also, as Dido
says, to institute this – which is a regulatory

00:50:05.479 --> 00:50:11.130
step in actually having a formal board to
system review meeting, which happened at the

00:50:11.130 --> 00:50:15.700
end of last week.
I think what I heard in the meeting was a

00:50:15.700 --> 00:50:21.400
real, clear sense from the system leaders
– because it wasn't just the trust, it was

00:50:21.400 --> 00:50:28.380
– as Dido says, it was the region but it
was also members from the STP, from neighbouring

00:50:28.380 --> 00:50:34.349
organisations, that there was a recognition
that actually it needed everybody to work

00:50:34.349 --> 00:50:39.200
together if they were going to play their
part in supporting improvements in care at

00:50:39.200 --> 00:50:43.039
the trust and for the population served by
the trust.

00:50:43.039 --> 00:50:49.550
So what we have done already is deployed an
improvement team of unprecedented scale to

00:50:49.550 --> 00:50:54.950
support the organisation. We've not done that
previously as NHS&nbsp;Improvement, it's been

00:50:54.950 --> 00:51:02.430
a single individual, or sometimes a small
group, so this is a much more comprehensive

00:51:02.430 --> 00:51:07.549
improvement offer, we've done that and are
in the process of doing that at the moment

00:51:07.549 --> 00:51:13.569
and that's to work alongside the staff in
the organisation, really to strengthen the

00:51:13.569 --> 00:51:16.839
pace in which they can address the failings
in care that have been identified.

00:51:16.839 --> 00:51:26.739
At the meeting, I think I also felt that it
was very clear that the new Chief&nbsp;Executive

00:51:26.739 --> 00:51:32.729
– and she has only been in post for a relatively
short period of time – that it was clear

00:51:32.729 --> 00:51:38.279
that she was very open in asking for support,
that she was working very closely with regional

00:51:38.279 --> 00:51:43.190
and national colleagues to identify what further
assistance was required and it was also clear

00:51:43.190 --> 00:51:48.690
that she was actively engaging with staff
to ensure that they were clear what was expected

00:51:48.690 --> 00:51:54.640
and what support was available.
Equally, I think I would agree with Dido absolutely

00:51:54.640 --> 00:51:59.190
and other colleagues may want to come in.
I think we came away very clear about the

00:51:59.190 --> 00:52:07.150
scale of the challenge and no one individual
will ever be able to deliver the sustained

00:52:07.150 --> 00:52:12.259
improvements that an organisation such as
Shrewsbury&nbsp;and&nbsp;Telford require, so the challenge

00:52:12.259 --> 00:52:17.690
for us, working, I think, very closely with
the regional team and with the trust and with

00:52:17.690 --> 00:52:24.709
the local system partners is to think now
about what additional support, over and above

00:52:24.709 --> 00:52:31.589
that improvement team I've talked about, we
can agree to put in place around that organisation

00:52:31.589 --> 00:52:38.970
to support them to be able to move at pace.
So we are exploring strategic solutions to

00:52:38.970 --> 00:52:44.819
put in some of that additional support and
the – I should say that the Chief&nbsp;Executive

00:52:44.819 --> 00:52:51.440
of the trust is fully supportive of that approach
and working with the region to identify those

00:52:51.440 --> 00:52:57.950
specific areas where another trust, in particular
a partnership with another organisation, would

00:52:57.950 --> 00:53:02.079
help them to accelerate the improvements that
they need to make.

00:53:02.079 --> 00:53:06.760
We are continuing to work very closely with
the CQC. We recognise that they play a very

00:53:06.760 --> 00:53:13.289
important role in ensuring that an organisation
delivers safe, high‑quality care. And I

00:53:13.289 --> 00:53:19.819
think, David, just to end really by saying
I would certainly want to use this opportunity

00:53:19.819 --> 00:53:27.079
to confirm our clear and unambiguous commitment
to enabling the organisation and system to

00:53:27.079 --> 00:53:32.770
address the issues identified by the CQC and
critically, to gain the confidence of the

00:53:32.770 --> 00:53:38.719
local population and those who use their services,
that they are receiving the standards of care

00:53:38.719 --> 00:53:44.269
that they can and should expect of the NHS.
&gt;&gt;Dido&nbsp;Harding: Amanda, thank you. Can we

00:53:44.269 --> 00:53:50.790
go to Steve to give us an update on the Ockenden&nbsp;Review?
&gt;&gt;Steve&nbsp;Powis: Yes, thank you Dido and of

00:53:50.790 --> 00:53:55.559
course I would want to echoes – sorry, echo
Amanda's words and offer my heartfelt sympathy

00:53:55.559 --> 00:54:00.739
to all those families who've suffered as a
result of poor maternity care and of course

00:54:00.739 --> 00:54:05.619
publicly state my thanks to all of them and
others who continue to strive for improvements

00:54:05.619 --> 00:54:10.410
in maternity services.
So, as the board is aware, an independent

00:54:10.410 --> 00:54:15.539
review, chaired by a midwife, Donna&nbsp;Ockenden,
was established in 2017 at the request of

00:54:15.539 --> 00:54:22.209
the then‑Secretary&nbsp;of&nbsp;State for Health&nbsp;and&nbsp;Social&nbsp;Care
to investigate the handling of 23&nbsp;cases relating

00:54:22.209 --> 00:54:28.789
to perinatal and paternal deaths at the trusts
and the board is also aware that, since then,

00:54:28.789 --> 00:54:33.490
further families have come forward to that
review and to the trust and that the trust

00:54:33.490 --> 00:54:40.200
has indeed identified other cases of potential
concern within its own records. And as of

00:54:40.200 --> 00:54:45.949
last week, the review has therefore widened
its scope over time and is now considering

00:54:45.949 --> 00:54:54.749
1,862 cases to see if they are the result
of poor care and this will be done in accordance

00:54:54.749 --> 00:55:00.180
with the updated terms of reference that were
published in November&nbsp;2019.

00:55:00.180 --> 00:55:06.839
Donna has now indicated, as she did – she
indicated last week that the review has now

00:55:06.839 --> 00:55:11.960
closed to further families, so – coming
forward, so that she can complete the work

00:55:11.960 --> 00:55:18.559
that is needed to be done in the review but
the trust will be able to deal with any remaining

00:55:18.559 --> 00:55:25.769
families who come forward with concerns and
they can be signposted to the trust but the

00:55:25.769 --> 00:55:30.969
task for Donna is now to complete the review
and ministers have asked her to complete a

00:55:30.969 --> 00:55:35.850
report by the end of this year.
I won't go into the other details in the paper

00:55:35.850 --> 00:55:43.890
but they do update the board on a recently‑launched
police investigation and also on the publication

00:55:43.890 --> 00:55:50.289
by ourselves, NHS&nbsp;Improvement, of a report,
or an investigation, that we launched following

00:55:50.289 --> 00:55:55.019
a complaint into the trust's handling of a
report they commissioned from the Royal&nbsp;College

00:55:55.019 --> 00:56:04.890
of Obstetricians and Gynaecologists in 2017.
So I'd leave it there and hand back to you,

00:56:04.890 --> 00:56:06.670
Dido.
&gt;&gt;Dido&nbsp;Harding: Thank you Steve. Ruth, is

00:56:06.670 --> 00:56:13.140
there anything that you would like to add?
&gt;&gt;Ruth&nbsp;May: Thank you very much, Chair. So,

00:56:13.140 --> 00:56:22.279
in line with Amanda and what Steve's done,
it's deeply, deeply distressing, what these

00:56:22.279 --> 00:56:30.589
families have had to go through and I recognise
that and extend my sympathies alongside my

00:56:30.589 --> 00:56:36.410
colleagues.
I guess I'm more – I guess I'm more disappointed

00:56:36.410 --> 00:56:45.039
in the most recent CQC inspections around
the fundamentals of care, that have been described

00:56:45.039 --> 00:56:49.069
as not being there, particularly around falls[?&nbsp;00.56.48]
prevention and pressure roles[?&nbsp;00.56.48]

00:56:49.069 --> 00:56:56.970
of care, both of which are fundamentals of
care. The trust does have more vacancies than

00:56:56.970 --> 00:57:05.450
a national average, so we've got – clearly
the trust has got more to do on that and so

00:57:05.450 --> 00:57:11.160
I'll be working with Amanda and Steve and
colleagues to further progress the strategic

00:57:11.160 --> 00:57:18.380
solutions along with yourself, Dido.
&gt;&gt;Dido&nbsp;Harding: Thank you Ruth. Unless there

00:57:18.380 --> 00:57:22.989
are any other comments, David, I think, on
behalf of the whole board, we should just

00:57:22.989 --> 00:57:28.390
formally note our deep sympathy and concern
for all the families that have been affected

00:57:28.390 --> 00:57:35.269
and sort of state our intention to do whatever
it takes to support the trust and the system

00:57:35.269 --> 00:57:39.799
to get back to the standards of care that
I know that the staff working there want to

00:57:39.799 --> 00:57:43.599
be able to deliver and we need to get there
as fast as we possibly can.

00:57:43.599 --> 00:57:47.319
&gt;&gt;David&nbsp;Prior: Thank you very much Dido and
I think it's definitely worth an update again

00:57:47.319 --> 00:57:50.990
from – at the next board meeting.
&gt;&gt;Dido&nbsp;Harding: Indeed.

00:57:50.990 --> 00:57:55.319
&gt;&gt;David&nbsp;Prior: Good, well thank you all very
much. Is there any other business that anyone

00:57:55.319 --> 00:57:59.630
would like to raise? Would – Laura&nbsp;Wade‑Gery,
do you have anything you would like just to

00:57:59.630 --> 00:58:09.339
say at the end or are you happy to bring it
to an end?

00:58:09.339 --> 00:58:14.460
Okay, Dido, is there anything you wanted to
just say at the end, before we sign off.

00:58:14.460 --> 00:58:17.930
&gt;&gt;Dido&nbsp;Harding: No, I'm good, thank you.
&gt;&gt;David&nbsp;Prior: No? Okay, good. Alright. Thank

00:58:17.930 --> 00:58:19.480
you all very much. Thank you.

